<u>26-AUG-2005 4:00 PM (Hong Kong Time)</u><br>
Congestion on the runway was bad at HK International Airport.  We sat in a hot, stuffy plane for 1 hour before we lifted off.  We were suppose to meet Michelle's Dad, Mr. Duty, at the airport in BKK and I had no way to let him know of the delay.
<p>
BKK is about ~1000km away from HK or about a 2 hr flight.  When we landed, apparently 2 other planes did too.  This caused a huge backup through immigration.  By the way, no sign of Marco.  People here are nice, but if you have an inch they take a foot and then some.  Case in point, Thai nationals would cut in line and then get others to cut in with them.  It took forever to get through.  I was a bit worried about our lugguge since we had spent at least 30 min in line, but it was there in one piece.
<p>
After a quick jaunt through customs, I was looking for Mr. Duty, whom I had no idea how he looked like except for a description that Michelle left me.  Look for a guy 6 ft tall with white hair.  Sure enough I saw someone that fit the description and stared at him hoping for some form of recognition.  See, I had emailed him our a pic of M and I so we could find each other.
<p>
Mr. Duty acknowledged me and we went for a taxi.  On the way, I was constantly being asked and pulled aside by taxi cab drivers asking if I needed a ride.    I ignored them.  Mr. Duty explained to me how yellow and green taxis were local BKK taxis, but any other color was outside of the city. This was required by law.
<p>
Keeping in mind the exchange rate is ~40 baht to 1 USD, the cab ride was relatively cheap at ~11 USD since Mr. Duty lives slightly outside of BKK.  He explained to me that there is a surcharge based on the amount of traffic during the day.
<p>
Going outside of the city where there was barely any English, I felt I got to experience a small slice of everyday life for a Thai native.  Tuk tuks were everywhere and loaded with people.  Tuk Tuks are basically pickup trucks with seats and a cover overhead for people to sit in.  You negotiate your price with the driver and if he agrees you jump in.  The other forms of traffic are scooters which zip in and out of traffice wherever room exists and passenger cars, but taxis and tuk tuks dominate the roads.  Taxis, while significantly higher then Tuk Tuks offer air conditioned comfort.  With temperatures around 32 deg C and 90% relative humity, it is easily welcome and worth the extra money.
<p> We arrived at Mr. Duty's condo and unpacked.  Mr. Duty showed us around.  We needed to do the laundry since we stank!  Luckily, Mr. Duty has a washer.  Dryers aren't common here.  Most people hang dry their clothes and that's what we did before crashing into bed.
